+
+### DICOM to MHA Conversion
+Converts DICOM series to MetaImage (MHD/RAW) format. Reads the first file for dimensions/spacing, initializes a numpy array, iterates through files to populate it, transposes axes from `(x, y, z)` to `(z, y, x)` to match DICOM storage, and saves using SimpleITK [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The dominant cost is reading and decoding DICOM headers and pixel data [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources. Additional memory is required for DICOM header parsing [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Loading speed depends heavily on I/O throughput and the efficiency of the DICOM parsing library (`bacaDicom` or similar) [S2, unofficial]. Conversion to density is a fast element-wise operation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles the complex coordinate transformations required for medical imaging (corner-point vs. center-point, axis flips) [S2, unofficial]. Correctly maps HU to physical density [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Assumes all slices have the same dimensions and orientation [S2, unofficial]. Does not handle missing slices or irregular spacing automatically without explicit `slice_distance` calculation [S2, unofficial]. Unsupported bit depths (e.g., 8-bit or 32-bit) are rejected [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When loading CT or MRI series for volume rendering where anatomical accuracy and correct physical scaling are critical [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** For non-medical volumetric data, or when real-time streaming of irregularly spaced slices is required without pre-processing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot R \cdot C)$, where $N$ is the number of slices, $R$ is rows, $C$ is columns. Linear with respect to total voxel count.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot R \cdot C)$ for storing the volume array.
+*   **Performance:** Loading speed depends on I/O throughput and DICOM parsing overhead. Conversion to density is computationally cheap. Orientation correction (rotation) adds minor overhead.
+*   **Strengths:** Handles standard medical imaging conventions (HU, spacing, orientation). Provides physically meaningful density values.
+*   **Limitations:** Assumes consistent slice ordering and spacing. Fails if `bytesAllocated` is not 16. Requires careful handling of coordinate system differences (DICOM vs. Renderer).
+*   **When to use:** When loading CT data for physical volume rendering or MPR.
+*   **When not to use:** For non-CT modalities without HU scaling, or when real-time streaming is required without full volume load.
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from implementations in medical imaging visualization libraries, specifically those integrating DICOM loading with WebGL/WebGPU volume rendering engines [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the specific challenges of rendering DICOM series in web-based environments, where standard image viewers often fail to represent the 3D nature of the data correctly (e.g., treating slices as cubes) [S1, unofficial]. The approach is documented in unofficial repositories such as `Panacea` (for the texture preparation and rendering pipeline) [S1, unofficial], `DGR` (for the DICOM loading and gradient-based processing) [S2, unofficial], and `ossium` (for the WebGPU-based DICOM loader and coordinate handling) [S3, unofficial].
+DICOM (Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine) is the standard for medical imaging data. Loading DICOM volumes requires parsing specific tags (PixelSpacing, SliceThickness, RescaleSlope) and handling the coordinate system differences between DICOM (patient-centered, middle-of-voxel) and typical graphics APIs (corner-of-voxel). This method bridges that gap, enabling accurate volume rendering of medical data. It is commonly used in medical visualization software, research prototypes, and clinical PACS viewers.
 
